    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "description",
      "value": "We will implement a numerical experiment to test the Algebraic Rigidity Hypothesis on small ReLU feedforward networks. We construct an analytical constraint set (1D function interpolation) where each constraint $i$ requires the network $f(x_i) = y_i$ at specific points $x_i$. We design constraints to explicitly probe the active ReLU region count, using a piecewise linear target function with $w$ kinks. For $w \\in [4, 64]$ in steps of 4, we vary hidden width $h$ from $w/2$ to $w$ and measure the minimal $h$ required to satisfy all constraints with error $\\leq 10^{-6}$. We track the number of active ReLU neurons during training to verify the 2 DOF per neuron claim and analyze constraint correlation via the Hessian of the loss landscape at convergence. This isolates the algebraic structure from generalization noise."
    },
    {
      "@type": "PropertyValue",
      "name": "expected_prediction",
      "value": "The minimal width $N_c(w)$ will scale linearly with $w$, specifically $N_c(w) \\approx \\lceil w/2 \\rceil$, confirming the hypothesis that each ReLU neuron provides 2 degrees of freedom (kink position and slope) for constraint satisfaction. Deviations will occur only when constraint correlation increases beyond a threshold, which will be identified via Hessian analysis."
    },
